UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 (Recommendation for Key Management), asymmetric encryption uses mathematically linked key pairs, a public key shared openly and a private key kept secret, enabling secure communication without prior key exchange. Two parties who have never communicated can exchange encrypted data using each other''s public keys.
Wrong (A): Asymmetric encryption protects data confidentiality during transmission, not physical hardware from theft, which requires physical security controls.
Wrong (C): Asymmetric encryption still uses encryption keys (public and private key pairs); it does not eliminate the need for keys entirely.
